-- allium: 3
-- daily-log.allium

-- Scope: The days of the bitácora — where what happens arrives before it has a
--   place, and what an inscription keeps saying about the day it came from
-- Includes: What makes a page a day, how a day comes into being, the day as the
--   landing place of what arrives without a stated destination, the appellation
--   of day that inscribed content carries, and where a day lands in the Markdown
--   mirror
-- Excludes:
--   - Calendars, time zones, and how a clock decides which day is today
--   - What a librarian judges worth inscribing, and how well it does it
--     (see agent-participation.allium)
--   - Editing and moving blocks (see block-editing.allium)
--   - The cascade by which spoken words become content (see voice-capture.allium)
-- Dependencies: Graph, Page, Block and Participant are governed by core.allium;
--   the hand behind an inscription by agent-participation.allium

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./block-editing.allium" as block

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The day is testimony, not a tray.
--
-- Things arrive before they have a place: a voice note, a thought with nowhere to
-- go yet, a librarian's account of what it did. They land on the day, and later
-- some of them are inscribed where they belong — in a page about the project, the
-- person, the idea. What this contract holds is that inscribing takes nothing
-- away. The day keeps what it received, exactly as it received it, because a day
-- that empties as it is filed stops being a record of what happened and becomes a
-- queue that happens to be dated.
--
-- The cost of the other design is not abstract: what a day held is the only
-- account of the order in which things occurred to someone, and it cannot be
-- reconstructed from the pages it fed.
contract TheDayIsTestimony {
    open_day: (graph: core/Graph, date: String) -> DailyLog
    inscribe: (participant: core/Participant, source: core/Block, into: core/Page) -> core/Block

    @invariant ADayIsNamedByItsDate
        -- A day's title is its date and nothing else. The title is not a label on
        -- the day, it is the day's identity: two pages for one date would split
        -- what happened in it, and a day renamed would move a testimony to a date
        -- on which it did not happen.

    @invariant TheDayKeepsWhatItReceived
        -- Inscribing content elsewhere never removes it from the day it arrived
        -- on. What was written on a day stays written on that day.

    @invariant InscribedContentNamesItsDay
        -- A block inscribed from a day says which day it came from. Without this
        -- the bitácora fills with statements whose moment is unrecoverable, and
        -- the day that fed them becomes an orphan nobody cites.

    @invariant ADayExistsBecauseSomethingArrived
        -- Days are not laid out in advance. A date with nothing on it has no
        -- page, because an empty day is not a fact about a life — it is a fact
        -- about a calendar, and Vera does not keep calendars.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A day of the bitácora.
--
-- Not a different kind of page: a page whose title is a date. Everything true of
-- a page is true of a day — its blocks, its revisions, its visibility, its links.
-- Modelling it as a separate thing would give the corpus two kinds of container
-- to reason about where it has one.
entity DailyLog {
    graph: core/Graph
    page: core/Page

    -- The date, written as the calendar writes it: `2026-08-03`. One spelling, so
    -- that ordering days is ordering their titles and no code has to guess a
    -- format. The Markdown mirror spells the same date `2026_08_03` in a filename
    -- because a colon-free, slash-free name is what a filesystem accepts; that is
    -- a fact about filenames, not a second format for dates.
    date: String
}

-- What an inscribed block says about the day it came from.
--
-- A separate thing from the block, for the same reason SpokenOrigin is separate
-- from the block it marks in voice-capture.allium: the content will be rewritten,
-- split and moved, and the day it came from will not change.
entity InscriptionOrigin {
    block: core/Block
    day: DailyLog

    -- The block on the day that this one was inscribed from, while it is still
    -- identifiable. Optional because the day's block may later be split or merged,
    -- and losing the exact source must not cost the block its day.
    source: core/Block?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A day opens because something arrived on it, and not before. The first thing
-- written on a date brings its page into being; nothing schedules it.
rule TheDayOpensWhenSomethingArrives {
    when: WriteOnDay(participant, graph, date, content)

    let membership = core/Membership{graph: graph, participant: participant}
    let existing = DailyLog{graph: graph, date: date}

    requires: exists membership
    requires: membership.status = active
    requires: is_date(date)

    ensures:
        if not exists existing:
            let page = core/Page.created(
                graph: graph,
                title: date,
                visibility: private
            )
            DailyLog.created(graph: graph, page: page, date: date)
            -- El día dice que es un día desde que nace, y lo dice como lo dice
            -- todo el corpus: con una propiedad. Es lo que permite preguntar por
            -- los días como clase —«qué escribí en julio»— sin reconocer una
            -- fecha en un título, y lo que hace que un día importado de otra
            -- parte y uno nacido aquí se parezcan.
            core/PropertyAssignment.created(
                graph: graph,
                page: page,
                key: "type",
                value: "bitácora"
            )
        core/Block.created(
            page: DailyLog{graph: graph, date: date}.page,
            parent: null,
            content: content
        )
}

-- Un día no puede dejar de ser un día.
--
-- La fecha del título y la propiedad dicen lo mismo, así que quitar la propiedad
-- no hace que la página deje de ser el 6 de agosto: hace que deje de contestar
-- cuando se pregunta por los días, y nada avisa. Lo que se pierde no es una
-- etiqueta sino la pertenencia a una clase, y se pierde en silencio.
--
-- El caso que lo trajo fue peor que eso. La superficie retiraba de un día el
-- aparato de poner propiedades —un día no lleva front matter, lleva su fecha—,
-- de modo que quitar el tipo dejaba la página sin ninguna forma de devolvérselo:
-- una puerta que sólo se abre hacia fuera. Por eso son dos obligaciones y no una:
-- el tipo no se quita, y poner propiedades sigue estando disponible.
rule ADayKeepsItsKind {
    when: core/ParticipantSubmits(participant, graph, origin_id, change, channel, evidence?)

    requires: change.kind = remove_property
    requires: change.property_key = "type"
    requires: exists DailyLog{graph: graph, page: change.page}

    ensures: change.rejected(
        reason: "un día es una bitácora; su tipo no se quita"
    )

    @invariant OtherPropertiesOfADayAreOrdinary
        -- Lo que se protege es la pertenencia a la clase, no el front matter
        -- entero. Cualquier otra propiedad de un día se pone, se edita y se quita
        -- como en cualquier página, y los demás valores de `type` que un día
        -- lleve escritos encima del suyo se editan igual: lo único que no se
        -- puede es dejar al día sin decir que es un día.

    @guarantee TheKindIsRestorableWhereItIsRemovable
        -- Ninguna superficie puede ofrecer quitar algo sin ofrecer ponerlo. Si un
        -- día enseña sus propiedades, enseña también cómo añadir una; si no
        -- enseñara ninguna de las dos cosas sería coherente. Lo que no puede
        -- existir es la mitad que resta.
}

-- Inscribing: taking what a day holds and writing it where it belongs.
--
-- The block that is born names the day, and the day's own block is not touched.
-- Both halves matter: the first is what makes the bitácora answerable about when
-- something was thought, the second is what keeps the day a testimony.
rule ParticipantInscribesFromADay {
    when: InscribeFromDay(participant, source, into, content)

    let membership = core/Membership{graph: into.graph, participant: participant}
    let day = DailyLog{page: source.page}

    requires: exists membership
    requires: membership.status = active
    requires: exists day
    -- A page cannot be inscribed into itself: that is editing the day, and
    -- editing a day is not inscribing.
    requires: into != source.page

    ensures:
        let block = core/Block.created(
            page: into,
            parent: null,
            content: content
        )
        InscriptionOrigin.created(block: block, day: day, source: source)
}

-- What the previous rule does not do, said out loud.
--
-- Stated as a rule rather than left to silence for the same reason as
-- agent/MovingLeavesTheHandAlone: "the day does not change here" is exactly the
-- kind of guarantee that gets implemented wrong by omission, and the wrong
-- implementation — a librarian that tidies the day as it files — looks like
-- diligence right up until the record is gone.
rule InscribingLeavesTheDayAlone {
    when: InscribeFromDay(participant, source, into, content)

    ensures:
        source.content = source.content
        source.page = source.page
}

-- A day cannot be renamed. Renaming it would say that what happened on the third
-- happened on the fourth, which is not a correction anyone is entitled to make.
rule ADayKeepsItsDate {
    when: core/ParticipantSubmits(participant, graph, origin_id, change, channel, evidence?)

    let day = DailyLog{page: change.page}

    requires: change.kind = rename_page
    requires: exists day

    refuses: "a day is named by its date; renaming it would move what happened to another day"
}

-- Splitting an inscribed block leaves both halves naming the same day, exactly as
-- splitting spoken content leaves both halves spoken.
rule TheDayFollowsASplit {
    when: block/BlockSplit(from, into)

    let origin = InscriptionOrigin{block: from}

    requires: exists origin

    ensures: InscriptionOrigin.created(block: into, day: origin.day, source: origin.source)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant OneDayPerDate {
    for day in DailyLogs:
        for other in DailyLogs:
            other.graph = day.graph and other.date = day.date implies other = day
}

invariant ADayIsItsTitle {
    for day in DailyLogs:
        day.page.title = day.date
}

-- The converse, which is what keeps the corpus from growing a second, silent kind
-- of day: a page titled like a date is a day, whoever made it and however.
invariant EveryDatedPageIsADay {
    for page in core/Pages:
        is_date(page.title) implies exists DailyLog{page: page}
}

invariant NoInscriptionWithoutItsDay {
    for origin in InscriptionOrigins:
        exists origin.day
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface DailyLogView {
    facing writer: core/Participant
    context day: DailyLog

    exposes:
        day.date
        day.page

    provides:
        WriteOnDay(writer, day.graph, day.date, content)
        InscribeFromDay(writer, source, into, content)

    contracts:
        fulfils TheDayIsTestimony

    @guarantee TodayIsAlwaysOneMoveAway
        -- Whatever is on screen, the day in progress can be reached without
        -- naming it. What arrives has to land somewhere immediately or it does
        -- not land at all, and a capture that costs a search is a capture that
        -- does not happen.

    @guarantee TheDayMirrorsUnderItsOwnRoof
        -- In the Markdown mirror a day is written under `journals/`, named for its
        -- date, and everything else under `pages/`. This is what makes the mirror
        -- legible to a reader who never heard of Vera, and what lets a corpus that
        -- came from Logseq go back the way it came.

    @guarantee WhatTheDayFedIsVisibleFromIt
        -- From a day one can see what was inscribed out of it. A testimony that
        -- cannot show what it produced invites the belief that nothing was done
        -- with it.

    @guarantee AContinuousJournalKeepsEachDaysEditingContext
        -- When several days are read together, editing an earlier day redraws
        -- that day in place. Saving never navigates to the leading day, changes
        -- the address, or discards the reader's position in the continuous log.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Which clock decides what today is, when the writer and the instance are in different time zones — and does a note written at 01:00 belong to the day that just began or the one that just ended?"
open question "Should a day show, block by block, what has already been inscribed and what is still waiting, or is a day that tracks its own filing no longer a testimony but a queue?"
open question "Does a day ever close — a point after which what happened on it can no longer be added to — or can a day be written to for ever?"
open question "When a librarian inscribes from a day, is the day itself entitled to a record of what the librarian did with it, beyond the account the librarian writes there by hand?"
